{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CT6p4aQogiEjP1wSnoLZFj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've read the full paper (11 pages) and the review context YAML. Now I need to dispatch two reviewers in a roundtable format:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nLet me first load the submit_dream_review_report tool, then dispatch both reviewers in parallel.\n\nLet me carefully organize the paper content I've read:\n\n**Title**: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics\n\n**Key contributions**:\n1. Theoretical characterization of detection power as function of n_items and contamination rate (inverted-U)\n\n\n2. Controlled calibration experiments on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B across 5 benchmarks, showing a 13pp AUROC advantage over ConStat and Min-K++ baselines\n3. Leaderboard audit of 310 models from Open LLM Leaderboard\n\n**Structure**:\n- §1 Intro\n- §2 Background (IRT/4PL, Person-Fit Statistics, Detection Methods hierarchy)\n- §3 IRT-FIT Framework (Detection Logic, Inverted-U Hypothesis, Calibration via Controlled Contamination)\n- §4 Simulation Studies (Power Simulation, Theoretical Power Derivation, Extended Benchmarks BBH/MATH)\n- §5 Controlled Contamination (Pilot, Full Experiment)\n- §6 Analysis (Baseline Comparison, 4PL vs 3PL, Post-RL Signal Retention)\n- §7 Leaderboard Audit (Data Collection, Flagged Rate, Temporal Trend, Gemini-1.5 Domain Analysis)\n- §8 Discussion\n- §9 Conclusion\n- References (9 references)\n\nNow I'm checking how the planned experiments align with what's actually covered—the extended simulation with high contamination and inverted-U characterization appears in §4.1 with Tables 1-2, the pilot contamination study in §5.1 with Table 6, the full controlled contamination experiment in §5.2 with Tables 7-9, theoretical power derivation in §4.2 with Tables 3-4, leaderboard reconnaissance in §7.1 with Table 15, the large-scale person-fit audit in §7.2-7.3 with Tables 16-18, and the 4PL versus 3PL comparison in §6.2 with Table 12.\n\nI'm also covering the baseline comparison across IRT-FIT, ConStat, and Min-K%++ in §6.1 with Tables 10-11, the multiscale lzm statistic in §6.4 with Table 14, the post-RL hypothesis test in §6.3 with Table 13, extended benchmarks on BBH and MATH in §4.3 with Table 5, and the MMLU domain-level analysis in §7.4 with Table 19. All twelve experiments are accounted for across the paper, so coverage looks solid.\n\nNow I'm ready to dispatch the two reviewers in parallel and start crafting their detailed prompts. First, I need to check the schema for the submit_dream_review_report tool to make sure I'm formatting everything correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38707,"cache_read_input_tokens":23426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38707},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7fb9d8d-04d9-47f4-8cd0-9727cb82002c","uuid":"1ea88b3f-c47f-4421-8ad5-3ed7a7ff4157"}
