{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HxVKT4DEDYpUtefUADvn6i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NiPzW7sLachpGggyyo95Rp","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment — Reviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Paper**: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics\n\n**Your focus**: Methods soundness, experimental design, statistical rigor, reproducibility.\n\n**Review standard**: Top ML venue, acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThe paper introduces IRT-FIT, a contamination detection framework operating at the minimal-information level (binary correct/incorrect responses only). It applies Item Response Theory person-fit statistics (specifically the standardized log-likelihood statistic lz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs. Key claims:\n\n1. **Theoretical characterization**: Detection power follows an inverted-U as a function of contamination rate, with power ∝ sqrt(n_items) × c × g(Δθ). Theory matches simulation with R²=0.85.\n2. **Controlled calibration**: Experiments on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B × 6 contamination levels × 5 benchmarks establish operating envelope: ≥15% contamination AND ≥5K items for AUROC≥0.70.\n3. **Power-information tradeoff**: 13pp AUROC gap vs ConStat, 16pp vs Min-K%++ — quantified as the cost of minimal information.\n4. **Leaderboard audit**: 310 models from Open LLM Leaderboard, 19% overall flagged rate, 18 models flagged on ≥3 benchmarks, temporal trend from 12%→25%.\n\n### Structure (11 pages, 19 tables, 9 references)\n- §1 Introduction, §2 Background (IRT/4PL, lz*, detection hierarchy)\n- §3 IRT-FIT Framework (detection logic, inverted-U hypothesis, controlled calibration design, multiscale lzm)\n- §4 Simulation Studies (power simulation Table 1-2, theoretical derivation Table 3-4, extended benchmarks BBH/MATH Table 5)\n- §5 Controlled Contamination (pilot Table 6, full experiment Tables 7-9)\n- §6 Analysis (baseline comparison Tables 10-11, 4PL vs 3PL Table 12, post-RL Table 13, lzm Table 14)\n- §7 Leaderboard Audit (data collection Table 15, flagged rates Table 16-17, temporal trend Table 18, Gemini-1.5 domain Table 19)\n- §8 Discussion, §9 Conclusion\n\n### Key Data Points for Your Review\n\n**Simulation (Table 1)**: Two-sided power at 15% contamination: MMLU(14042)=0.98, HellaSwag(10042)=0.73, GSM8K(1319)=0.58, ARC-C(1172)=0.13, HumanEval(164)=0.24. Inverted-U R² mean=0.80.\n\n**Theory (Table 3-4)**: Theory vs sim R²: negative regime 0.91, positive 0.83, combined 0.85. Min n_items for power 0.8 at 15%: 8,200.\n\n**Controlled Contamination (Tables 6-9)**: Pilot: Δlz*=+5.3 at 15% (d=1.2, p=.028), Δlz*=-4.3 at 50% (signal reversal). Full: AUROC peaks at 15% (MMLU: 0.79), no condition exceeds 0.80. Cross-model direction agrees 5/5 benchmarks, magnitude ratio 1.13-1.33. SFT generalization: directly trained items +29pp accuracy, same-domain untrained +3pp.\n\n**Baselines (Tables 10-11)**: IRT-FIT mean AUROC@15%=0.72, ConStat=0.84, Min-K%++=0.90. Gap=-0.13 vs ConStat.\n\n**4PL vs 3PL (Table 12)**: 2-3pp power improvement, ~1.5pp FPR reduction, lz* correlation 0.97.\n\n**Post-RL (Table 13)**: Signal attenuation 51%, AUROC drops 0.79→0.65, p=.08 (marginal).\n\n**Leaderboard (Tables 15-19)**: 310 models analyzed, 280 with per-item data + 30 supplemented. Flagged rate: MMLU 22%, GSM8K 28%, overall 19%. Temporal Spearman ρ=0.38. Cross-benchmark mean ρ=0.35.\n\n### Your Evaluation Dimensions\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the application of IRT person-fit statistics to LLM contamination detection well-justified? Are there violations of IRT assumptions (local independence, unidimensionality) that undermine the approach?\n2. **Simulation design**: Is the contamination simulation realistic? (Setting random items to correct vs. actual SFT behavior)\n3. **Controlled experiment design**: Are 2 model families × 6 levels × 5 benchmarks sufficient? Is QLoRA SFT a valid proxy for real-world contamination?\n4. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are confidence intervals / effect sizes properly reported? Is 3 seeds enough?\n5. **Baseline comparison fairness**: Is the comparison with ConStat and Min-K%++ fair? Are they run on the same ground-truth data?\n6. **Leaderboard audit validity**: Is the threshold calibration from 2 models transferable to 310 diverse models? What about false positive control?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Are there enough details to reproduce? Is code/data availability mentioned?\n8. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's absent that would strengthen the paper?\n\nPlease provide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)","reviewer_id":"1","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":63300,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7fb9d8d-04d9-47f4-8cd0-9727cb82002c","uuid":"c49281f7-4418-4f3a-9535-90de3d1c7901"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NiPzW7sLachpGggyyo95Rp","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group::reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7fb9d8d-04d9-47f4-8cd0-9727cb82002c","uuid":"bc043164-24d0-4f8d-ab9e-825e5e508aee","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:12:16.080Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group::reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group::reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HxVKT4DEDYpUtefUADvn6i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013Y8R43RKR3sVvd8hDPBcAL","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment — Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Paper**: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics\n\n**Your focus**: Novelty, positioning, related work completeness, framing, writing quality, contribution significance.\n\n**Review standard**: Top ML venue, acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThe paper introduces IRT-FIT, applying IRT person-fit statistics (lz*) — established in psychometrics for detecting exam cheating — to LLM benchmark contamination detection. The key positioning: IRT-FIT operates at the \"minimal-information\" level (binary responses only), unlike ConStat (needs rephrased retesting), Min-K%++ (needs token probabilities), or n-gram methods (need training data/weights). The paper establishes an \"operating envelope\" (≥15% contamination, ≥5K items) and quantifies the cost of minimal information as a 13pp AUROC gap.\n\n### Structure (11 pages, 19 tables, 9 references)\n- §1 Introduction, §2 Background (IRT/4PL model, lz* statistic, detection frontier hierarchy)\n- §3 IRT-FIT Framework (detection logic, inverted-U hypothesis, controlled calibration, multiscale lzm)\n- §4 Simulation Studies, §5 Controlled Contamination, §6 Analysis (baselines, ablations)\n- §7 Leaderboard Audit, §8 Discussion, §9 Conclusion\n\n### Key Claims and Framing\n\n**Contribution 1 — Theoretical characterization**: Power ∝ sqrt(n_items) × c × g(Δθ), inverted-U with detection trough at 40-70%. Theory-simulation R²=0.85.\n\n**Contribution 2 — Controlled calibration**: Operating envelope ≥15% contamination + ≥5K items. No condition achieves AUROC>0.80.\n\n**Contribution 3 — Power-information tradeoff**: Positions IRT-FIT on a \"detection frontier\" organized by information access level. 13pp AUROC gap framed as explicit, quantified cost rather than weakness.\n\n**Contribution 4 — Leaderboard audit**: 310 models, 18 multi-benchmark flagged, temporal trend. Claimed as uniquely enabled by minimal-information operation (retrospective audit of historical submissions).\n\n### Related Work Cited (9 references total)\n1. Snijders 2001 — lz* statistic foundation\n2. Hong et al. 2021 — multiscale lzm\n3. Shi et al. 2024 — Min-K%++ (MIA)\n4. De Koninck et al. 2024 — ConStat\n5. Oren et al. 2024 — Time Travel probing\n6. Shi et al. 2026 — CoDeC (in-context effects)\n7. Wang et al. 2025 — MIA fails post-RL\n8. Zhou et al. 2023 — benchmark contamination problem\n9. Yang et al. 2023 — rephrased samples\n10. PSN-IRT 2025 — item parameter source\n\n### Your Evaluation Dimensions\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is applying IRT person-fit to LLM contamination? Is this a straightforward application of existing psychometric tools, or does it involve non-trivial adaptation? Is the inverted-U observation itself a contribution?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing related works? Consider:\n   - Other IRT applications to LLMs (e.g., Uto 2024, Polo et al. 2024 on IRT for LLM evaluation)\n   - Other contamination detection methods not cited (e.g., benchmark-specific detectors, embedding-based methods)\n   - Educational measurement literature on person-fit beyond Snijders\n   - The PSN-IRT work itself — how dependent is IRT-FIT on PSN-IRT's item parameters?\n3. **Framing and positioning**: Is \"minimal-information operating point\" a compelling framing? Does the \"detection frontier\" concept add value or is it post-hoc rationalization of lower performance? Is the \"operating envelope\" characterization genuinely useful?\n4. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is it appropriately scoped for 8 pages + references? Are claims properly hedged? Any overclaims?\n5. **Contribution significance**: Given that AUROC never exceeds 0.80 and the method is outperformed by baselines, is the contribution sufficient for a top venue? Is the \"honest characterization of limitations as contribution\" framing convincing?\n6. **Reference count**: Only 9 references for a paper touching IRT, contamination detection, LLM evaluation — is this adequate?\n7. **Broader impact**: Does the paper adequately discuss ethical implications of flagging specific models (e.g., Gemini-1.5)?\n\nPlease provide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)","reviewer_id":"2","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":63300,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7fb9d8d-04d9-47f4-8cd0-9727cb82002c","uuid":"022aaffb-b1a2-448e-8856-cce3639994b6"}
